A return to roots.

I joined Mozilla as a backfill for a design manager going on maternity leave. The project was a take on a new web browser, but wasn’t for Firefox. The internet feels broken, full of noise and browsers weren’t helping. They were building a product and needed help getting over the finish line. 

This was very compelling to me.personally and professionally. My first web design job was building SaaS applications, and Firefox (and crucially Firebug) were critical to me being able to learn front-end development. I had a strong love for the company, and their mission. It felt a little but like coming home. 

It was codenamed Pro Client. It’s premise was simple: 

We’re long overdue for a better way to use the internet. Browsers were created to simplify access to the online world. It has come a long way since the 1990s, but it’s still not optimized for creativity, concentration or a sense of calm. A web browser built for today’s context wouldn’t be a browser. Flowstate believed that the right tools, organized in the right way, can help you feel better while using it. The product was named Flowstate. 

The design team for the project included several UX designers and a motion designer. The overall team was large with PM and software developers and research. They had an existing sprint schedule I aligned to. The job was running the weekly design meeting remotely. Checking status, reviewing progress for the week, and setting up breakout times to huddle and work together. We would use the early week to make progress, and then align and review with PM’s before the broader team review. 
 
There were many unique features of the browser, but my favorite had to be ‘Set Aside’. It would take all the tabs and set them aside for later. It was a single button and everything was moved aside for later. It seemed like a feature missing from all web browsers. 

